name: errors and files
intro: Things fail. A file is not there, the disk is full, the input is
intro: junk. In C nothing stops you when that happens -- a call fails,
intro: hands back a sign of it, and the program carries on with garbage
intro: unless you looked. So you look, every time.
intro:
intro: Each library call says how it fails: `fopen` returns NULL, `malloc`
intro: returns NULL, `fread` returns fewer items than you asked for.
intro: When a call fails it also sets `errno` (from <errno.h>), a number
intro: saying why. `strerror(errno)` turns it into words; `perror("x")`
intro: prints `x: ` plus those words to stderr. Those words differ between
intro: C libraries, so never compare them -- print your own message.
intro:
intro: Read a file, and check the open:
intro:
intro:     FILE *f = fopen(path, "rb");
intro:     if (f == NULL) {
intro:         fprintf(stderr, "prog: cannot open %s\n", path);
intro:         return 1;
intro:     }
intro:     char buf[4096];
intro:     size_t n;
intro:     while ((n = fread(buf, 1, sizeof buf, f)) > 0)
intro:         fwrite(buf, 1, n, stdout);
intro:     fclose(f);
intro:
intro: `fread` and `fwrite` move raw bytes, count included, so they are
intro: safe for data with zero bytes in it; `fgets` and `puts` are not.
intro: `fread` returning 0 means end of file or an error: `ferror(f)` tells
intro: you which.
intro:
intro: Once a function owns several things (a file, a buffer) every early
intro: `return` has to release all of them. The tidy way is one exit:
intro:
intro:     int status = 1;
intro:     ...
intro:     if (bad) goto cleanup;
intro:     status = 0;
intro: cleanup:
intro:     free(buf);
intro:     if (f != NULL) fclose(f);
intro:     return status;
intro:
intro: Start every owned thing as NULL so cleanup can free what exists.
intro: In your own functions, return 0 for success and nonzero for failure,
intro: the way programs do.
intro:
intro: `fputs(s, f)` writes a string to any stream exactly as it is. Unlike
intro: `puts` it adds no newline, so `fputs("bad input\n", stderr)` sends a
intro: fixed message to standard error.
---
q: fdump.c -- copy a file to standard output
spec: Take exactly one argument, a file name. Copy the file's bytes to
spec: standard output unchanged, zero bytes and a missing final newline
spec: included, and exit 0.
spec:
spec: If the file cannot be opened print `fdump: cannot open NAME` to
spec: standard error (NAME is the argument as given) and exit 1. Do not
spec: print the strerror text. With any other number of arguments print
spec: `usage: fdump FILE` to standard error and exit 2.
file: fdump.c
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: 
prog: int main(int argc, char **argv)
prog: {
prog: 	if (argc != 2) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "usage: fdump FILE\n");
prog: 		return 2;
prog: 	}
prog: 	FILE *f = fopen(argv[1], "rb");
prog: 	if (f == NULL) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "fdump: cannot open %s\n", argv[1]);
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	}
prog: 	int status = 0;
prog: 	char buf[4096];
prog: 	size_t n;
prog: 	while ((n = fread(buf, 1, sizeof buf, f)) > 0) {
prog: 		if (fwrite(buf, 1, n, stdout) != n) {
prog: 			fprintf(stderr, "fdump: write failed\n");
prog: 			status = 1;
prog: 			break;
prog: 		}
prog: 	}
prog: 	if (ferror(f))
prog: 		status = 1;
prog: 	fclose(f);
prog: 	return status;
prog: }
case: printf 'a\nb' > f; fdump f
case: printf 'x\0y\n' > f; fdump f | wc -c
case: fdump @txt@ | wc -l
case: fdump nothere.txt; echo $?
case: fdump; echo $?
case: printf 'a\n' > f; fdump f f; echo $?
hint: fopen can return NULL, fread hands back how many bytes it got, and fwrite those same bytes -- never treat the data as text
